---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import FoundationsLayout from "../../components/FoundationsLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";
import { excerpt } from "../../lib/excerpt";
import workspaceModule from "../../../../src/watershed/workspace.gleam?raw";
import workspaceBeam from "../../../../src/watershed/workspace_beam.gleam?raw";
import workspaceTests from "../../../../test/watershed/workspace_beam_test.gleam?raw";

const channelFields = excerpt(
  workspaceModule,
  "pub fn manifest_field(",
  "pub fn layout_field(",
  "pub fn connections_field(",
);
const manifestEntry = excerpt(workspaceModule, "pub type ManifestEntry {");
const snapshotFn = excerpt(workspaceModule, "pub fn snapshot(");
const preparationState = excerpt(
  workspaceModule,
  "pub type PreparationState(subtree) {",
);
const prepareFn = excerpt(workspaceModule, "pub fn prepare(");
const deleteFn = excerpt(workspaceBeam, "pub fn delete_instance(");
const preservationTest = excerpt(
  workspaceTests,
  "pub fn workspace_lifecycle_converges_and_preserves_deleted_data_test() -> Nil {",
);
---

<FoundationsLayout
  slug="workspaces"
  description="A watershed workspace persists component instances, layout, and port connections beneath one child map, then derives a catalog-aware view while preserving data the local host cannot use."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>One root slot opens the whole workspace</h2>
    <p>
      A composed app still has one typed document root. Give the workspace one
      <code>ChildField</code> there, and it owns three channels below it: a map
      of component instances, a sequence for presentation order, and a
      sequence of port connections.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={channelFields} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      The JavaScript and BEAM adapters create those channels while they are
      detached, wire their handles into the workspace map, then attach the
      complete subtree through the root. A peer never needs three magic keys
      at the application root, and component data never shares the workspace's
      control namespace.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>The manifest points at data; it doesn't swallow it</h2>
    <p>
      Each manifest value names the stable instance and component version,
      carries its config as JSON, and embeds a handle to that component's own
      child map. The config can vary by component kind. The child map remains
      a separate collaborative structure with one owner.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={manifestEntry} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Handle discovery walks nested JSON. Writing this record therefore
      attaches the child map before the manifest operation reaches another
      client. A late joiner reads the same entry and resolves the same map.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Stored data and usable data are different lists</h2>
    <p>
      Collaborative state can outlive the code that wrote it. A manifest can
      name a component this build doesn't carry. A layout can repeat an
      instance. Two independently safe graph edits can form a cycle after
      merge. Deleting those records on sight would turn one client's ignorance
      into everybody's data loss.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>workspace.snapshot</code> keeps the raw values, decodes what it can,
      removes duplicate or dangling layout references from the effective view,
      and asks <code>port_graph.effective</code> for the deterministic acyclic
      graph. Every rejected value comes back as a diagnostic. Nothing gets
      rewritten.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={snapshotFn} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2><code>Prepared</code> stops before <code>Ready</code></h2>
    <p>
      Persistence can answer three questions without pretending it ran a
      component: does the catalog know this exact kind and version, does the
      config decode, and does the child map resolve? A successful answer is{" "}
      <code>Prepared</code>. An unsupported version is{" "}
      <code>Unavailable</code>. Bad stored data or config is{" "}
      <code>Failed</code>. A handle still in flight stays{" "}
      <code>Loading</code>.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={preparationState} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={prepareFn} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Prepared is not running.</strong> The next layer calls the
        descriptor's start closure, installs subscriptions, and reaches{" "}
        <code>Ready</code>. Keeping the names separate makes a startup failure
        impossible to hide behind a resolved handle.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Deletion removes reachability, not history</h2>
    <p>
      One delete touches three channels, so order matters. The adapter removes
      every layout reference, removes every connection that reaches the
      instance, and deletes the manifest key last. Repeating the operation
      finishes a partial attempt instead of failing on already-missing data.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={deleteFn} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Watershed doesn't have channel garbage collection. The instance map
      stays attached and disappears only from workspace reachability. A caller
      that retained its handle can still read it—the test pins that behavior
      instead of calling the orphan “deleted.”
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={preservationTest} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
      <figcaption class="annot">
        source: test/watershed/workspace_beam_test.gleam
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h3>The runtime boundary is still real</h3>
    <p>
      These modules persist and prepare instances. They don't execute a
      delivery, mutate a target component, mount a view, or keep a component
      subscription alive. That shell comes next; the workspace gives it one
      stable snapshot to reconcile.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">The workspace</span>
    <p>
      One child map owns the manifest, layout, and wiring. Raw collaborative
      data stays intact; the local catalog derives the view it can safely use.
      From here, <a href="/guide">the survey procedure</a> returns to the
      document runtime watershed ships end to end today.
    </p>
  </div>
</FoundationsLayout>
